## Broken fragment links

Symptom: `quaydoc check` reports `broken-fragment`, or clicking a table of
contents entry never scrolls the page.

The most common cause is a mismatch between the ids the TOC assigns to
headings and the fragments the reference machinery computes for the same
title. Both sides of the pipeline are documented as needing to agree
exactly; when they disagree, every link to the headings concerned breaks.
Start with the page whose fragment is reported, look at the heading titles
involved, and compare the two anchor computations in the source. The
regression usually arrives in a release whose changelog mentions anchor ids.

## Missing pages

`missing-page` means a path link points at a source file that does not
build a page. Check the spelling, and remember that `draft: true` files
build nothing.

## Duplicate ids

`duplicate-id` means one page assigns the same id twice — usually two
headings with the same title. The first keeps the base id; the second gets
a `-2` suffix.

## Nothing renders

Check the front matter: a missing closing `---` swallows the whole page.
Run `python3 -m quaydoc.cli check site` and read `.quay-check.txt`.
